{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-01-mean-price-alligation-ratio",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Mean Price and the Alligation Ratio",
    "description": "Understand alligation as a weighted-average shortcut and turn price gaps into a mixing ratio.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with two equal-price bags to make mean price concrete, then make the quantities unequal so the learner sees why the mean moves toward the heavier ingredient. Derive the cross-difference rule from that balance, practise reading the ratio in the correct direction, convert ratios to quantities, and finish by applying the same idea to concentration percentages.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach mean price as the cost of one unit of the finished mixture and show why it must lie between the two ingredient prices. Build the rule of alligation from weighted-average reasoning before naming the cross-difference shortcut. Cover cheaper : dearer = (dearer price − mean price) : (mean price − cheaper price), interpreting why the mixture contains more of the ingredient whose price is closer to the mean. Convert an alligation ratio into actual quantities when the total amount or one ingredient quantity is given. Include integer, decimal, and percentage-strength versions so learners see that the same weighted-average idea works for prices and concentrations. Learners should check a ratio by recomputing the weighted mean rather than trusting the cross alone."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn why price gaps can tell us a mixing ratio.\n\nImagine rice at ₹20/kg and ₹30/kg mixed to sell at ₹24/kg. The final price must sit between the two — and because ₹24 is closer to ₹20, the mixture should contain more of the cheaper rice.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two bags of rice cost ₹20/kg and ₹30/kg.\n\nIf we mix 1 kg of each, the 2 kg mixture costs ₹50 in total.\n\nSo one kilogram of the mixture costs:\n₹50 ÷ 2 = ₹25.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That ₹25 is the mean price: the cost of one unit of the finished mixture.\n\nWith positive quantities, the mean must sit between the two ingredient prices.\n\nHere: ₹20 < ₹25 < ₹30.\n\nThat simple check catches many impossible answers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
